Trusted Types is a browser API that prevents DOM-based XSS by requiring that dangerous DOM sinks (innerHTML, document.write, eval) only accept TrustedHTML objects — never raw strings.

Without Trusted Types, this code is vulnerable: element.innerHTML = userInput; // attacker controls userInput.

With Trusted Types enforced (via CSP: require-trusted-types-for 'script'), this line throws a TypeError — raw strings are rejected. You must use a Trusted Types policy with explicit sanitization:

const policy = trustedTypes.createPolicy('default', {
  createHTML: (input) => DOMPurify.sanitize(input)
});
element.innerHTML = policy.createHTML(userInput);

Trusted Types make DOM XSS impossible by default — the developer must explicitly opt into dangerous operations through a policy.
